   130	
   131	- Return the learned artifact with a machine-readable receipt; independent replay is mandatory.
   132	- Bind cutoff identity/time, maximum source/label-availability times, partitions, selected parameters, metrics, and gate predicates.
   133	- Bind source hashes, transitive implementation hashes, model identity, imported helpers, and a cutoff-bound cache key.
   134	- Never reuse caches across cutoffs, sources, or implementations; later issue models/caches cannot serve earlier issues.
   135	- Every research contract and PI/adversarial review must inventory transitive historical-learning functions.
   136	- Review cutoff receipt, maximum admitted times, partition derivation, cross-cutoff cache exclusion, and imported-helper bindings.
   137	- Verify post-cutoff mutation invariance through selection, support, tickets, and routing; label-free final output alone does not prove causality.
   138	
   139	### Recency and shared implementation
   140	
   141	- Treat recency weighting as learning: pass policies explicitly and select them only inside prior cutoff windows.
   142	- Policies include equal expansion, time/issue decay, bounded recent-state blends, change detectors, refit cadence, half-life, window, blend, threshold, and ESS floors.
   143	- Receipts must bind the full weight ledger, effective sample size (ESS), long-history shrinkage, and policy parameters.
   144	- Sparse recent evidence must fail closed or shrink to the frozen long-history prior.
   145	- Recent-state blends must equal `(1-alpha) * long_history + alpha * recent_state`, with both distributions normalized and component masses reconciled separately.
   146	- Multiplying recent rows within one global normalization is not an accepted blend.
   147	- All chronology machinery must come from `packages/beidan/inst/python/beidan_backend/temporal_learning`, including R/QMD and imported-function usage.
   148	- Research clients supply domain fit/predict/select callbacks only; do not copy cutoff, availability, partition, weighting, ESS, cache, receipt, runner, or replay utilities.
   149	- New direct historical fitting outside shared learning contexts and duplicated temporal helpers fail governance checks.
   150	- Replace/delete obsolete implementations without compatibility wrappers.
   151	- Static enforcement scans `src/` and `scripts/`; allow legacy debt only by exact AST occurrence fingerprint and count, never whole-file exemptions.
   152	
   153	## Persistent data layer (recorded 2026-10-06)
   154	
   155	- Analytical datasets are Parquet on the NAS at `/mnt/nas/Data/beijing-lot` (CIFS automount; env `BEIDAN_PARQUET_ROOT`); short-lived data stays on NVMe. Read through the package, never by opening files directly: `beidan::read_dataset()` (Arrow, typed, per-file sha256 verified) and `beidan::dataset_query()` (DuckDB, read-only). `dataset_query()` registers **one table per call**, so cross-table joins go in R.
   156	- Single data entry: `beidan_backend.catalog` — `paths.py` resolves roots and `tier:relative/path` tokens, `registry.py` reads `registry/catalog.json` and `schemas/<schema_id>.json`. Roots fail closed when unset or absent; do not add a machine default or a second path convention.
   157	- One version lifecycle for base datasets and derived artifacts: `beidan_backend.versioning.publish_version()` writes `versions/<label>/` and points `current.json` at the newest. Identical content is a no-op; a label already published with different content is refused; refreshing an older label never moves the pointer backwards. `content_digest()` hashes data files only, so a new `generated_at` does not change identity.
   158	- `settlement` = base dataset (19 tables, registered in the version manifest) plus derived `play_labels` (one row per issue/slot/play for all five plays, registered in the catalog, partitioned by `play`). Partition arguments are not interchangeable: `issue_year` selects a base partition, `play` selects a derived shard, `issue` filters rows; misuse stops.
   159	- **This CIFS mount corrupts in-place overwrites.** Writing over an existing file produced zero-padded/truncated results: a Parquet with a zeroed footer, and twice a JSON with trailing NULs. Always write to a temp name, read it back, then rename. `versioning.write_json_atomically()` and `settlement_backfill.write_parquet_atomically()` exist for this; never use a plain `write_text`/`write_parquet` onto an existing path here.
   160	- Sandisk is being physically retired by the owner; the raw capture layer is not retained (derived layers carry sha256 references). Do not add work that depends on the Sandisk disk.
   161	
   162	## Local verification environment (recorded 2026-10-03)
   163	
   164	- This workspace is worked on over remote SSH: the Positron client is local, but the workspace, R/Python sessions, and the Posit Assistant extension host run on the server (`~/.positron-server`). Assistant configuration therefore lives on the server — global `~/.posit/assistant/settings.json` and project `.posit/assistant/settings.json` — not on the local machine. `modelTiers` (which also selects the conversation-summary/compaction model, on the `low` tier) is config-file-only; there is no `assistant.*` VS Code setting for it.
   165	- Project Python interpreter is `.venv/bin/python` (pytest 9.0.3); R is `/opt/R/4.6.1/bin/R`. Python code imports `beidan_backend`, so run Python tests with `PYTHONPATH=packages/beidan/inst/python:$PWD`; some `tests/` areas additionally need `src/` and their own subdirectory on `sys.path`. Several `tests/` directories fail collection for lack of a root `conftest.py`; this is a pre-existing harness gap, not a code failure.
   166	- Verify a suspected regression with an A/B swap of the changed implementation (restore the pre-change files, rerun, restore, hash-check) instead of assuming any failing suite is new.
